 *** Resource timeout (%p) in %ws:%s

        The resource is owned shared by %d threads
     The resource is owned exclusively by thread %p
 

 *** Critical Section Timeout (%p) in %ws:%s

                The resource is unowned.  This usually implies a slow-moving machine due to memory pressure

   Go determine why that thread has not released the critical section.

   The critical section is owned by thread %p.
    

 *** Inpage error in %ws:%s

         The critical section is unowned.  This usually implies a slow-moving machine due to memory pressure

   This failed because of error %Ix.

     The instruction at %p referenced memory at %p.
 This means the data could not be read, typically because of a bad block on the disk.  Check your hardware.

    This means the machine is out of memory.  Use !vm to see where all the memory is being used.

  

 *** An Access Violation occurred in %ws:%s

 This means that the I/O device reported an I/O error.  Check your hardware.     read from       write to        an invalid address, %p

        The instruction at %p tried to %s       

 *** A stack buffer overrun occurred in %ws:%s

      a NULL pointer

                If this bug ends up in the shipping product, it could be a severe security hole.
               This is usually the result of a memory copy to a local buffer or structure where the size is not properly calculated/checked.
  

